  "narrative": "The metabolite panel is strongly enriched for **Biological oxidations** (Reactome R-HSA-211859; FDR = 0.0, fold enrichment = 128.5) and its child pathway **Phase II – Conjugation of compounds** (R-HSA-156580; FDR = 0.0, fold enrichment = 159.1). A secondary signal appears in **Phase I – Functionalization of compounds** (R-HSA-211945; FDR = 1 × 10⁻⁶) and the broader **Xenobiotics** module (R-HSA-211981; FDR = 2 × 10⁻⁶). These five Reactome entries collectively implicate a coordinated shift in drug/xenobiotic metabolism, consistent with either environmental exposure, pharmacological intervention, or a pathological state that disrupts Phase I/II equilibrium.\n\n**Key driver metabolites**\n\n- **L-Cysteine (C00097)** is a critical node: it is the direct precursor for glutathione (γ-glutamyl-cysteinyl-glycine) synthesis, and via membership in the Phase II – Conjugation pathway it anchors the conjugating arm of the response. The co-occurrence plausibility score across all nine observed metabolites is 1.0, confirming that the observed compounds are biologically coherent rather than random. **beta-Mercaptoethanol (C00928)** reinforces this sulfur-handling theme and also maps to Phase II conjugation.\n\n- **Benzo[a]pyrene-7,8-dihydrodiol-9,10-oxide (C14853)** and **N-hydroxy-4-aminobiphenyl (C20319)** are confirmed members of Phase I and Phase II branches. C14853 is specifically annotated to glutathione conjugation, indicating that a potentially DNA-damaging diol epoxide is being shunted toward detoxifying conjugation. C20319 additionally appears in N-acetylation and cytosolic sulfonation pathways, reflecting the multi-pathway processing of aromatic amines.\n\n- **Methimazole (C07190)** and **bromide (C01324)** map to Phase I functionalisation, consistent with their roles as substrates or modulators of cytochrome-P450 activity.\n\n**Biological significance**\n\nThe convergence on Phase I/II metabolism suggests the treatment or disease state alters xenobiotic 